"SPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1)\nciteturn30951view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1\",\"lineno\":211}); Total lines: 703\nL186: SAR  | 0.333  | 0.163  | 0.334  | 0.164  | 0.324  | 0.160  | 0.314  | 0.151  | 0.326  | 0.159\nL187: O-LoRA  | 0.334  | 0.163  | 0.303  | 0.148  | 0.300  | 0.147  | 0.299  | 0.145  | 0.309  | 0.151\nL188: EWC-LoRA  | 0.337  | 0.163  | 0.335  | 0.165  | 0.328  | 0.163  | 0.323  | 0.157  | 0.331  | 0.162\nL189: SPRInG (Ours)  | 0.346  | 0.188  | 0.353  | 0.192  | 0.351  | 0.191  | 0.351  | 0.190  | 0.350  | 0.190\nL190:  | Review Writing\nL191:  | Period 1  | Period 2  | Period 3  | Period 4  | Period Avg\nL192: Methods  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L\nL193: Base  | 0.301  | 0.125  | 0.302  | 0.125  | 0.303  | 0.125  | 0.303  | 0.125  | 0.302  | 0.125\nL194: RAG each  | 0.273  | 0.122  | 0.275  | 0.124  | 0.278  | 0.123  | 0.279  | 0.125  | 0.276  | 0.123\nL195: RAG accum  | 0.274  | 0.122  | 0.272  | 0.122  | 0.277  | 0.124  | 0.278  | 0.125  | 0.275  | 0.123\nL196: PAG each  | 0.289  | 0.120  | 0.290  | 0.122  | 0.285  | 0.121  | 0.281  | 0.120  | 0.286  | 0.121\nL197: PAG accum  | 0.286  | 0.120  | 0.288  | 0.120  | 0.288  | 0.121  | 0.279  | 0.120  | 0.285  | 0.120\nL198: OPPU each  | 0.304  | 0.124  | 0.307  | 0.125  | 0.310  | 0.126  | 0.307  | 0.125  | 0.307  | 0.125\nL199: OPPU accum  | 0.310  | 0.123  | 0.312  | 0.123  | 0.308  | 0.122  | 0.306  | 0.123  | 0.309  | 0.123\nL200: CAMA  | 0.305  | 0.125  | 0.313  | 0.126  | 0.317  | 0.125  | 0.314  | 0.124  | 0.312  | 0.125\nL201: SAR  | 0.309  | 0.124  | 0.316  | 0.124  | 0.314  | 0.124  | 0.308  | 0.123  | 0.312  | 0.124\nL202: O-LoRA  | 0.306  | 0.125  | 0.301  | 0.125  | 0.304  | 0.125  | 0.304  | 0.126  | 0.304  | 0.125\nL203: EWC-LoRA  | 0.304  | 0.124  | 0.315  | 0.125  | 0.319  | 0.124  | 0.312  | 0.124  | 0.312  | 0.124\nL204: SPRInG (Ours)  | 0.325  | 0.141  | 0.326  | 0.142  | 0.329  | 0.143  | 0.324  | 0.142  | 0.326  | 0.142\nL205: #### Baselines.\nL206: We compare SPRInG against several baselines categorized as follows: (1) Base – generating responses using the vanilla LLM without any personalization or fine-tuning. For standard personalization baselines (2-4), we evaluate two setups: each (using only current period data) and accum (using cumulative data from period 0 to current period).\nL207: These include: (2) RAG (cite64†Hwang et al., 2023 ) – augmenting input with retrieved history from the respective corpus; (3) PAG (cite79†Richardson et al., 2023 ) – utilizing summarized user profiles generated from the corresponding data scope; and (4) OPPU (cite65†Tan et al., 2024 ) – fine-tuning user-specific LoRA adapters (cite75†Hu et al., 2022 ) either locally on the current period or sequentially on the cumulative stream.\nL208: We also include continual learning baselines: (5) CAMA (cite72†Kim et al., 2024 ) – a replay-based method that selectively buffers high-confidence samples exceeding the previous period’s average confidence and mixes them with historical data for training; (6) SAR (cite80†Seo et al., 2025 ) – a method that prioritizes replay samples from under-represented clusters based on effective use-frequency to mitigate overfitting to dominant patterns; (7) O-LoRA (cite81†Wang et al., 2023 ) – a method that projects new parameter updates onto a subspace orthogonal to previous adapters to reduce interference; and (8) EWC-LoRA (cite82†Xiang et al., 2023 ) – a method that applies elastic weight consolidation with Fisher Information to penalize drastic changes in parameters critical for prior periods.\nL209: #### Implementation details.\nL210: In our experiments, we utilize Gemma-3-4B-IT (cite83†Team et al., 2025 ) as the backbone LLM. For training, we employ the AdamW (cite84†Loshchilov and Hutter, 2019 ) with a weight decay of 0.01 and a learning rate of $1\\times 10^{-4}$, utilizing a linear decay scheduler with a warmup ratio of 0.1. We use a per-device batch size of 4, train for 2 epochs per period, and truncate the maximum sequence length to 768 tokens.\nL211: For LoRA, we target the query and value projection layers with a rank $r=8$, scaling factor $\\alpha=8$, dropout of 0.05, and no bias. During inference, we ensure deterministic outputs using greedy decoding with a repetition penalty of 1.2, setting the maximum generated tokens to 600.\nL212: Regarding the hyperparameters for SPRInG, we set the balancing term $\\alpha$ in the drift score function (Eq. cite85†1 ) to 1.0 and construct the drift set by retaining the top 30% ($p=30$) of samples. For the residual replay buffer, we maintain a maximum budget of $N_{\\max}=50$ samples per user.\nL213: During inference, we retrieve $k=2$ documents using BM25 (cite86†Robertson et al., 2004 ) as Sim and apply a relevance gating threshold $\\sigma$, defined as the task-specific third quartile (Q3) of the retrieval score distribution aggregated across all users and periods, to filter out less relevant contexts. Finally, we set the mixing coefficient $\\lambda$ for logit interpolation (Eq. cite87†8 ) to 0.5 to balance the parametric and non-parametric distributions equally.\nL214: ### 4.2 Main results\nL215: Table cite88†1 presents the evaluation results of SPRInG against baselines on Abstract Generation and Review Writing. First, SPRInG consistently achieves the best performance across all evaluation periods for both tasks, with gains both in ROUGE-1 and ROUGE-L metrics. To be specific, in Abstract Generation, our method achieves relative improvements of 3.86% in ROUGE-1 and 15.85% in ROUGE-L over the strongest CL baseline.\nL216: Similarly, in the Review Writing task, SPRInG maintains a lead over existing competitive approaches, achieving improvement of 4.49% in ROUGE-1 and 13.60% in ROUGE-L. These results indicate that our proposed approach effectively balances plasticity and stability, delivering substantial performance gains over the strongest existing methods.\nL217: Furthermore, we observe that standard personalization approaches (e.g., RAG, PAG) can be ineffective or even detrimental in certain contexts. Notably, in the Review Writing task, both the Each and Accum variants of RAG and PAG fail to surpass the non-personalized Base model. For instance, RAG each results in an 8.60% drop in ROUGE-1 compared to the Base model, indicating a vulnerability to noisy or irrelevant context.\nL218: However, SPRInG overcomes this limitation, demonstrating an 18.12% relative improvement over RAG each. This suggests that SPRInG selectively leverages beneficial context while filtering irrelevant history.\nL219: Finally, CL baselines show greater stability than the naive accumulation strategy. We observe a general trend where replay-based approaches (e.g., CAMA, SAR) tend to yield robust performance, slightly outperforming or matching regularization-based methods like O-LoRA. However, even these strong baselines fall short of SPRInG. This limitation likely stems from treating historical samples uniformly or relying on fixed heuristics, which are not designed to prioritize drift-relevant update.\nL220: In contrast, SPRInG achieves superior results by explicitly targeting and adapting to meaningful preference drifts, allowing for more efficient and effective personalization.\nL221: Table 2: Effectiveness of selective adaptation. Impact of selective data usage within period-wise updates. Comparing whole-data training with partial adaptation using different selection criteria.\nL222: Methods  | Abstract Generation  | Review Writing\nL223: --- | --- | ---\nL224: ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L\nL225: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL226: All-100%  | 0.300  | 0.146  | 0.309  | 0.123\nL227: Random-30%  | 0.312  | 0.152  | 0.312  | 0.125\nL228: Bottom-30%  | 0.284  | 0.142  | 0.309  | 0.124\nL229: Top-30% (Ours)  | 0.324  | 0.155  | 0.313  | 0.125\nL230: ### 4.3 More Analyses\nL231: #### Ablation study on training dynamics.\nL232: To validate the efficacy of selective adaptation (Sec. cite9†3.1 ), we analyze the impact of training data volume and selection criteria in Table cite89†2 . First, regarding data usage, our Top-30% strategy consistently outperforms the All-100% baseline across tasks (e.g., +8.0% relative ROUGE-1 gain in Abstract Generation).\nL233: This indicates that indiscriminately training on the entire interaction history introduces noise or redundant information, whereas focusing on a subset of drift samples enhances adaptation efficiency. Second, regarding the selection criteria, we observe a clear performance hierarchy: Top-30% > Random-30% > Bottom-30%. Notably, the Bottom-k strategy results in the lowest performance, falling even below the Random baseline.\nL234: This decline suggests that samples with low drift scores correspond to redundant interactions already internalized by the adapter. Consequently, forcing updates on them provides minimal utility or may even act as noise that interferes with the learning process. Collectively, these results confirm that our scoring function effectively selects high-value interactions essential for training the model with evolving user preferences.\nL235: Table 3: Effectiveness of interpolation. Performance comparison across different weighting factors ($\\lambda$). The coefficient $\\lambda$ determines the relative reliance on retrieved context versus the model’s internal parametric knowledge.\nL236: Methods  | Abstract Generation  | Review Writing\nL237: --- | --- | ---\nL238: ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L\nL239: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL240: $\\lambda=0.0$  | 0.324  | 0.155  | 0.313  | 0.125\nL241: $\\lambda=0.1$  | 0.334  | 0.187  | 0.326  | 0.142\nL242: $\\lambda=0.5$ (Ours)  | 0.350  | 0.190  | 0.326  | 0.142\nL243: $\\lambda=1.0$  | 0.294  | 0.154  | 0.276  | 0.112\nL244: \nL245: Figure 3: Interaction scalability of SPRInG. Performance comparison on different interaction history lengths.\nL246: #### Ablation study on inference dynamics.\nL247: Table cite90†3 compares different interpolation coefficients $\\lambda$ from Eq. cite87†8 , which balance the knowledge from parametric adapter and the non-parametric replay buffer at inference time.\nL248: Overall, relying on a single-source is suboptimal, suggesting that two memory components provide complementary signals; the parametric path ($\\lambda=0.0$) robustly captures persistent user traits, but can miss sparse, instance-specific details that remain in the residual buffer; conversely, the non-parametric path ($\\lambda=1.0$) is vulnerable to retrieval noise and partial mismatches, which can directly interfere with generation, especially in the more diverse Review Writing stream.\nL249: As a result, moderate interpolation yields the best performance across tasks: in Abstract Generation, $\\lambda=0.5$ improves ROUGE-L by 22.58% over the parametric-only baseline and 23.38% over the non-parametric-only setting. In Review Writing, it achieves gains of 13.60% and 26.79%, respectively. Notably, small $\\lambda$ provides gains (e.g., $\\lambda=0.1$), whereas over-reliance on retrieval degrades performance ($\\lambda=1.0$), supporting our choice of $\\lambda=0.5$ as a balanced default.\nL250: #### Robustness across the number of interactions.\nL251: To investigate the adaptability of SPRInG to varying scales of user data, we categorized users into two groups: Small Interactions and Large Interactions. Detailed statistics regarding interaction distribution for each group are provided in Table cite91†5 (see Appendix cite37†B.2 ).\nL252: The Small Interactions group represents a scenario of relative data sparsity, whereas the Large Interactions group includes users with extensive histories, presenting challenges related to noise accumulation and retrieval efficiency. Figure cite92†3 illustrates the performance comparison across these groups. In Abstract Generation, SPRInG demonstrates robust scalability, consistently outperforming baselines with large gains in both settings.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImproving Chain-of-Thought for Logical Reasoningvia Attention-Aware Intervention (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09805v1)\nciteturn30951view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09805v1\",\"lineno\":262}); Total lines: 901\nL253:   * Bugliarello and Okazaki (2020) E. Bugliarello and N. Okazaki Enhancing machine translation with dependency-aware self-attention. In Proceedings of the 58th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, D. Jurafsky, J. Chai, N. Schluter, and J. Tetreault (Eds.), Online, pp. 1618–1627. External Links: cite99†Link†aclanthology.org , cite100†Document†dx.doi.org Cited by: cite101†§3 .\nL254:   * Cobbe et al. (2021) K. Cobbe, V. Kosaraju, M. Bavarian, M. Chen, H. Jun, L. Kaiser, M. Plappert, J. Tworek, J. Hilton, R. Nakano, C. Hesse, and J. Schulman Training verifiers to solve math word problems. CoRR abs/2110.14168. External Links: cite102†Link , 2110.14168 Cited by: cite103†§4.1 .\nL255:   * Dubey et al. (2024) A. Dubey, A. Jauhri, A. Pandey, A. Kadian, A. Al-Dahle, A. Letman, A. Mathur, A. Schelten, A. Yang, A. Fan, A. Goyal, A. Hartshorn, A. Yang, A. Mitra, A. Sravankumar, A. Korenev, A. Hinsvark, A. Rao, A. Zhang, A. Rodriguez, A. Gregerson, A. Spataru, B. Rozière, B. Biron, B. Tang, B. Chern, C. Caucheteux, C. Nayak, C. Bi, C. Marra, C. McConnell, C. Keller, C. Touret, C. Wu, C. Wong, C. C. Ferrer, C. Nikolaidis, D. Allonsius, D. Song, D. Pintz, D. Livshits, D. Esiobu, D. Choudhary, D.\nL256: Mahajan, D. Garcia-Olano, D. Perino, D. Hupkes, E. Lakomkin, 